# =============================================================================
# stub.ps1 - the UNIVERSAL STEADFAST BOOTSTRAPPER (public thin installer)
#
# Standard: STEADFAST-DEVKIT-STANDARD.md sec6b.3 (universal stub) + sec4a (licensing)
#           + sec6a (hub request bus) + sec6b.2 (signing / published release hashes).
# Doctrine: D31 THE-BUS-CARRIES-REQUESTS-NEVER-AUTHORITY /
#           D32 LICENSING-AND-SIGNING-ARE-LOCAL.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   The ONE public download for every Steadfast product. It is a thin, native
#   Windows bootstrapper (PowerShell + WinForms, compiled to a small signed .exe
#   by PS2EXE - see build.md). It carries NO product code and NO key material.
#   It knows only: which product it is, that product's license worker URL, and
#   how to verify + run whatever the gated feed hands back.
#
# THE FLOW (sec6b.3)
#   1. Fingerprint this machine -> HWID (byte-identical to the product's own
#      Node os-based scheme, so the signed token binds and the app activates).
#   2. Show a minimal "request a license" form (name / email / optional company).
#   3. POST name+email+HWID to  https://<LicenseDomain>/request   -> requestId.
#   4. Poll  https://<LicenseDomain>/poll/<requestId>  until the operator acts.
#   5a. APPROVED  -> the poll returns the LOCALLY-signed license token. Use it as
#       Authorization: Bearer to pull the real installer from the gated feed
#       https://<LicenseDomain>/updates/<product>/... , VERIFY its published
#       SHA-512, RUN it, and write license.json so the real app opens
#       pre-activated.
#   5b. REJECTED / no approval -> fetch NOTHING. The downloaded stub stays an
#       empty shell. That is the security goal (denied == nothing to reverse).
#
# WHY NO SIGNATURE VERIFY HERE
#   The worker's /updates proxy already verifies the Ed25519 license (product +
#   HWID + expiry) with the PUBLIC key BEFORE it will proxy a single byte
#   (license is the key, worker is the door). The installed app re-verifies the
#   token locally against its BUNDLED public key on first run. So the stub needs
#   no key material at all: it does an integrity check (published hash) on the
#   binary and a light, non-security payload-binding sanity check for good UX.
# =============================================================================

Set-StrictMode -Off
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
[Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol = [Net.SecurityProtocolType]::Tls12

Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Windows.Forms
Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Drawing

